---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';

const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/parleq/parleq-speech';
---

<Layout title="DIY: SSO & Sign-in with Google at home — Parleq" description="Wire up OIDC sign-in or Sign-in-with-Google for Parleq's cloud cleanup at home, without an IT department. A path-picker decision table, a flagship Sign-in-with-Google → Vertex walkthrough, the gotchas that burn hours, and how to drive it with an AI assistant.">
  <SiteHeader active="docs" />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 pt-16 pb-24">
    <p class="text-sm">
      <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/'} class="text-[color:var(--color-text-subtle)] hover:text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">&larr; Docs</a>
    </p>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[color:var(--color-accent)]">Enterprise &middot; At home</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl leading-[1.1] font-medium tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">
      DIY: SSO &amp; Sign-in with Google
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[17px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      You don&rsquo;t need a corporate IdP to use Parleq&rsquo;s OIDC sign-in. The same engine works for a single person on a free identity provider or a personal Google account &mdash; no per-user API keys, no long-lived cloud secret on disk.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Who this page is for:</strong> hobbyists and geeks wiring this up without an IT department &mdash; probably with an AI assistant open in another window. This stuff is fiddly, and the console UIs change constantly. So this page gives you the <em>route map</em> and the gotchas that burn hours; your assistant fills in the exact console-clicking for whatever the current UI looks like. The section at the bottom on <a href="#assistant" class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">working with your AI assistant</a> is the intended workflow, not an afterthought.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] p-5">
      <p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
        Setting this up for a company instead? The IT-admin version &mdash; Okta / Entra ID / any OIDC IdP, with MDM pins &mdash; lives in <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/enterprise-sso/'} class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Enterprise SSO</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Pick your path -->
    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Pick your path
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Three routes, all roughly $0 &mdash; Cognito&rsquo;s free tier, OAuth, STS, and Workforce Identity are all free; you pay only for the LLM tokens you actually use. Start at the top.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)]">
      <table class="w-full text-[14px] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
        <thead class="border-b border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Path</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">What it needs</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Effort</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-[color:var(--color-border)]">
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3"><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">A. Sign in with Google &rarr; Vertex / Gemini</strong> <span class="ml-1 rounded bg-[color:var(--color-accent-soft)]/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[11px] font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)]">Recommended start</span></td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">A GCP project. No broker, no STS, no organization. The simplest thing that works.</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Lowest</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3"><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">B. Cognito &rarr; AWS Bedrock</strong></td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">A free Cognito user pool as your personal IdP; an IAM role assumed via <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code>.</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Medium</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3"><strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">C. Cognito &rarr; GCP Workforce Federation &rarr; Vertex</strong></td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Everything in B, <em>plus</em> a Google Cloud <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">organization</strong> (Cloud Identity Free + a domain you own). The heaviest path.</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Highest</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Choose <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">A</strong> unless you have a specific reason not to. Choose <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">B</strong> if you want Bedrock (Claude / GPT-OSS) and are comfortable in the AWS console. Choose <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">C</strong> only if you specifically want the federation architecture &mdash; it needs a GCP org, which is the single biggest step on this whole page.
    </p>

    <!-- Path A walkthrough -->
    <h2 class="mt-14 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Path A &mdash; Sign in with Google &rarr; Vertex
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      &ldquo;Sign in with Google, dictate with Gemini.&rdquo; No broker, no Workforce Identity Federation, no gcloud CLI. GCP refuses to accept Google itself as a workforce IdP, so federation can&rsquo;t do this &mdash; instead Parleq&rsquo;s own Google sign-in requests the <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">cloud-platform</code> scope, and the resulting OAuth <em>access</em> token is a valid Vertex bearer directly (exactly what gcloud ADC produces, done in-app). <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">No GCP organization required.</strong>
    </p>

    <ol class="mt-6 space-y-7 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)] tabular-nums shrink-0">1.</span>
        <div>
          <p class="text-[color:var(--color-text)] font-medium">Create a GCP project and enable the Vertex AI API.</p>
          <p class="mt-2">Any project works. Enable <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Vertex AI API</strong> from the API Library, and note the <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Project ID</strong> (the ID, not the display name).</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)] tabular-nums shrink-0">2.</span>
        <div>
          <p class="text-[color:var(--color-text)] font-medium">Create an OAuth client &mdash; of the <em>iOS</em> type.</p>
          <p class="mt-2">This is the non-obvious one. Parleq&rsquo;s sign-in uses a <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">custom-scheme redirect</strong>, so create an <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">iOS</strong>-type OAuth client (public, no secret) and use its <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">reversed client ID</strong> as the redirect scheme. A Desktop-type client with a loopback redirect is on the roadmap but not the path today.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)] tabular-nums shrink-0">3.</span>
        <div>
          <p class="text-[color:var(--color-text)] font-medium">Grant IAM on the project.</p>
          <p class="mt-2">The signing account needs <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">roles/aiplatform.user</code> and <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">roles/serviceusage.serviceUsageConsumer</code> on the project. If you&rsquo;re the project Owner, you already have both.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)] tabular-nums shrink-0">4.</span>
        <div>
          <p class="text-[color:var(--color-text)] font-medium">Write the config.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] p-4">
      <pre class="text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)] whitespace-pre overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "oidc": {
    "issuer": "https://accounts.google.com",
    "client_id": "123456789-abc.apps.googleusercontent.com",
    "scopes": ["openid", "email", "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform"],
    "redirect_uri": "com.googleusercontent.apps.123456789-abc:/oauth2redirect",
    "extra_auth_params": { "access_type": "offline", "prompt": "select_account consent" }
  },
  "vertex": {
    "project": "your-project",
    "region": "us-central1",
    "auth_mode": "googleOAuth"
  }
}`}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Why <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">"prompt": "select_account consent"</code> is the default to use, not just <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">"consent"</code>: <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">select_account</code> prevents a dead-end where Google silently reuses whatever account is active in the browser and hard-blocks the sign-in without ever showing an account picker. <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">consent</code> guarantees Google re-issues a refresh token (it only does so when it shows the consent screen). Together they make sign-in reliable across the consent-screen modes below.
    </p>

    <!-- The consent-screen trilemma -->
    <h3 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">The consent-screen trilemma</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      This is the #1 source of confusion. Your OAuth consent screen is in one of three publishing states, and each has a different trade-off:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Testing mode</strong> &mdash; works immediately, but refresh tokens expire every <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">7 days</strong>. You&rsquo;ll re-sign-in roughly weekly. Fine for kicking the tires.</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Published / External</strong> &mdash; refresh tokens persist, but because <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">cloud-platform</code> is a restricted scope an unverified personal client shows an &ldquo;unverified app&rdquo; interstitial. Click <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Advanced &rarr; continue</strong>; harmless for a personal client. Why does a stranger who somehow got your client ID gain nothing? The tokens belong to whoever <em>signs in</em>, and the IAM grant is on <em>your</em> project &mdash; their token can&rsquo;t touch it. Worst case, Google suspends a client that&rsquo;s being abused.</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Internal</strong> &mdash; the clean endgame: no warning, no token cap, no expiry, and outsiders are hard-blocked. But it requires putting the project in a Google Cloud <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">organization</strong> (Cloud Identity Free + a domain you own), and you then sign in with an <em>org</em> account, not <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">@gmail.com</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Pragmatic call: start in Testing to prove it works, then Publish (External) and click past the warning if weekly re-sign-in annoys you; go Internal only if you already want a GCP org for other reasons.
    </p>

    <!-- Granular-consent gotcha -->
    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 p-5">
      <p class="text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-amber-900">
        <strong class="font-semibold">Gotcha that burned us live &mdash; tick the box.</strong> Google&rsquo;s granular-consent screen shows &ldquo;See, edit, configure and delete your Google Cloud data&rdquo; as an <strong>unchecked checkbox</strong>. If you don&rsquo;t tick it, you get a working sign-in whose token then <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">403</code>s at Vertex &mdash; and the next silent refresh may sign you out entirely. Parleq now detects a missing grant at sign-in and tells you, but the fix is simple: <strong>tick the box</strong> on the consent screen.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Path B -->
    <h2 class="mt-14 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Path B &mdash; Cognito &rarr; AWS Bedrock
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      A free Amazon Cognito user pool becomes your personal OIDC issuer; an IAM role trusts it via <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code>. Cognito gives you an AWS-hosted public issuer with a publicly-fetchable JWKS, so the AWS leg works with nothing exposed to the internet yourself. The free tier covers far more monthly active users than a single person will ever use.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <li>&bull; Create a user pool and add an <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">app client</strong> of the <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">public client</strong> type (no secret), with the authorization-code grant and a callback of <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">parleq-auth://oidc/callback</code>.</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Scopes:</strong> <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">["openid", "email"]</code>. Don&rsquo;t add <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">profile</code> &mdash; the wizard-created client usually doesn&rsquo;t enable it, and requesting it makes Cognito error-redirect instantly (the symptom is a window that flashes open and shut; see troubleshooting). Cognito also rejects <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">offline_access</code> but still issues a refresh token from the auth-code grant.</li>
      <li>&bull; In IAM, register an <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">OIDC identity provider</strong> for the pool issuer (<code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">https://cognito-idp.&lt;region&gt;.amazonaws.com/&lt;pool-id&gt;</code>) with the app client ID as an audience.</li>
      <li>&bull; Create a role whose trust policy admits that provider via <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code> with an <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aud</code> condition equal to the client ID. Attach a permissions policy granting <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">bedrock:InvokeModel*</code>.</li>
      <li>&bull; Config: <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aws.auth_mode</code> = <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidc</code>, plus <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aws.role_arn</code> and <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">aws.region</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] p-4">
      <pre class="text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)] whitespace-pre overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "oidc": {
    "issuer": "https://cognito-idp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/us-east-1_EXAMPLE",
    "client_id": "EXAMPLEcognitoclientid",
    "scopes": ["openid", "email"]
  },
  "aws": {
    "region": "us-east-1",
    "auth_mode": "oidc",
    "role_arn": "arn:aws:iam::<account>:role/ParleqDictation"
  }
}`}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      The trust-policy and permissions-policy shapes are in <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/enterprise-sso/'} class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Enterprise SSO &rarr; AWS Bedrock setup</a> &mdash; the same JSON, just with your Cognito issuer as the federated principal.
    </p>

    <!-- Path C -->
    <h2 class="mt-14 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Path C &mdash; Cognito &rarr; GCP Workforce Federation &rarr; Vertex <span class="ml-1 align-middle rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] border border-[color:var(--color-border)] px-2 py-0.5 text-[11px] font-medium text-[color:var(--color-text-subtle)]">Advanced</span>
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      This is the full federation architecture for Vertex, and the heaviest path on the page. Choose it only if you specifically want workforce federation &mdash; Path A reaches Vertex with far less ceremony. The blocker is that Workforce Identity Pools are an <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Organization-level</strong> GCP resource, so you need a Google Cloud org first.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <li>&bull; Build the Cognito pool exactly as in Path B (it&rsquo;s your issuer for both clouds).</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Get a GCP organization:</strong> sign up for <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Cloud Identity Free</strong> with a domain you own and complete the DNS TXT verification. This is the real work in Path C.</li>
      <li>&bull; Create a <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">workforce pool</strong> and add an <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">OIDC provider</strong> for the Cognito issuer. The issuer URI <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">must be public HTTPS</strong> &mdash; a localhost IdP cannot work here even if you upload the JWKS inline. Cognito is public HTTPS, so it&rsquo;s fine.</li>
      <li>&bull; Grant <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">roles/aiplatform.user</code> to the workforce-pool <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">principalSet</strong> (scope it tighter by attribute if you can).</li>
      <li>&bull; Config: <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertex.auth_mode</code> = <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">oidcFederation</code>, plus <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertex.workforce_provider</code> (<code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">locations/global/workforcePools/&lt;pool&gt;/providers/&lt;provider&gt;</code>) and <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">vertex.project</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Full workforce-pool details (attribute mapping, the <code class="rounded bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[14px]">x-goog-user-project</code> billing requirement) are in <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/$/, '') + '/docs/enterprise-sso/'} class="font-medium text-[color:var(--color-accent)] underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Enterprise SSO &rarr; Google Cloud Vertex AI setup</a>.
    </p>

    <!-- Working with your AI assistant -->
    <h2 id="assistant" class="mt-14 scroll-mt-20 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Working with your AI assistant
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      This page is the map; your assistant is the turn-by-turn navigation. The console UIs move around too fast for any doc to keep exact click paths current, but an assistant can generate them for whatever you&rsquo;re looking at right now.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Paste this page in</strong> and tell it which path (A, B, or C) you picked. Ask it to generate the exact console steps for the current UI.</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Capture as you go:</strong> the client ID, the redirect URI, the role ARN, pool / provider resource names, and the project ID. These are the values the config wants and the values your assistant will keep asking for.</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Paste exact error text back in.</strong> Most failures here have a precise cause; the literal error string is what lets the assistant pinpoint it.</li>
      <li>&bull; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Use the connection doctor.</strong> Parleq&rsquo;s <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Settings &rarr; Company Account &rarr; Test connection</strong> tells you which hop failed (IdP / token exchange / provider) with the server&rsquo;s reason &mdash; exactly the detail your assistant needs to fix it. Feed that output in verbatim.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Troubleshooting -->
    <h2 class="mt-14 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--color-text)]">
      Troubleshooting
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)]">
      <table class="w-full text-[14px] text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
        <thead class="border-b border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Symptom</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Cause / fix</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-[color:var(--color-border)]">
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3">&ldquo;Unverified app&rdquo; warning at sign-in</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Expected on a Published/External Google client requesting the restricted <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">cloud-platform</code> scope. Click <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Advanced &rarr; continue</strong> &mdash; harmless for your own personal client.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3">&ldquo;App can only be used within its organization&rdquo;, and no account picker appeared</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Google silently reused the active browser session. Add <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">"prompt": "select_account consent"</code> to <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">extra_auth_params</code> so it always shows the picker.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]"><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="font-mono text-[12px]">403</code> at Vertex after a successful sign-in</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Either you didn&rsquo;t tick the granular-consent checkbox (re-sign-in and tick it), or the signing account is missing <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">roles/aiplatform.user</code> + <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">roles/serviceusage.serviceUsageConsumer</code> on the project.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Signed out shortly after signing in</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">The refresh was rejected &mdash; usually a partial grant (missing the consent checkbox) or a Testing-mode client whose 7-day refresh token expired. Re-sign-in; tick the box; consider Publishing the consent screen.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-bg)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Cognito sign-in window flashes open and shut instantly</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="font-mono text-[12px]">invalid_scope</code> &mdash; you requested a scope the app client doesn&rsquo;t have enabled. Drop <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">profile</code> (and don&rsquo;t request <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">offline_access</code>); use <code class="font-mono text-[12px]">["openid", "email"]</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="bg-[color:var(--color-surface)]">
            <td class="px-4 py-3">GCP workforce provider create fails: &ldquo;issuer must be HTTPS&rdquo;</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3">Path C&rsquo;s issuer must be public HTTPS. A localhost IdP can&rsquo;t work even with inline JWKS &mdash; use the Cognito issuer (it&rsquo;s public HTTPS).</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </article>

  <footer class="border-t border-[color:var(--color-border-subtle)]">
    <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-5xl flex-col items-start justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-10 text-sm text-[color:var(--color-text-subtle)] sm:flex-row sm:items-center">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-base font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Parleq</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>Apache-2.0</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6">
        <a href={repoUrl} class="hover:text-[color:var(--color-text)]">GitHub</a>
        <a href={`${repoUrl}/releases`} class="hover:text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Releases</a>
        <a href={`${repoUrl}/blob/main/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md`} class="hover:text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Licenses</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </footer>
</Layout>
